{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0127wNzu3psYc2UumajLuB8A","type":"tool_result","content":"9:## 1. The map: every instance on record placed by its excess (item 01, 2026-10-31)\n49:### 1.1 Findings\n144:### 1.2 Kill verdict\n153:### 1.3 What this does not claim\n164:### 1.4 Figure\n174:### 1.5 Tables, in full\n887:## 2. `opt / n` against the excess: one parameter, or two? (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n935:### 2.1 Findings\n1024:### 2.2 Kill verdict\n1035:### 2.3 What this does not claim\n1055:### 2.4 Figure\n1068:### 2.5 Tables, in full\n1282:## 3. The forest regime, by brute force (item 03, 2026-11-01)\n1378:### 3.1 Findings\n1484:### 3.2 Kill verdict\n1504:### 3.3 What this does not claim\n1526:### 3.4 Tables, in full\n1881:### 3.5 The two censored random block trees, rerun for an hour each\n1903:## 4. Industrial instances: the 24 SCOOP instances in the excess (item 04, 2026-11-01)\n1984:### 4.1 Findings\n2090:### 4.2 The planner's statement (a draft of the paper's paragraph)\n2134:### 4.3 Kill verdict\n2144:### 4.4 What this does not claim\n2165:### 4.5 Tables, in full\n2302:## 5. `β⁺` by simulation: the width of explicit constructions at large `n` (item 05, 2026-11-01)\n2382:### 5.1 Findings\n2504:### 5.2 Kill verdict\n2521:### 5.3 What this does not claim\n2544:### 5.4 Figure\n2554:### 5.5 Tables, in full\n2877:## 6. The forest regime, on paper (item 06, 2026-11-01)\n2929:### 6.1 Statements\n2984:### 6.2 Proofs\n3181:### 6.3 The brute-force check\n3287:### 6.4 Findings\n3330:### 6.5 Lean-ready statements for item 11\n3363:### 6.6 Kill verdict\n3370:### 6.7 What this does not claim\n3393:### 6.8 Tables, in full\n3419:## 7. `β⁺` on paper: the provable constructions analysed (item 07, 2026-11-01)\n3464:### 7.1 The layer lemma (deterministic)\n3497:### 7.2 The exploration recursion for the layer fractions\n3578:### 7.3 Greedy independent sets, and the slot picture\n3641:### 7.4 The recursions against simulation\n3713:### 7.5 `β⁺(d, μ)` on the uniform model, beside `β*`, `b` and the rule\n3752:### 7.6 Findings\n3827:### 7.7 Tables, in full\n3938:### 7.8 Kill verdict\n3952:### 7.9 What this does not claim\n/-\nCopyright (c) 2026 Alexandre Linhares. All rights reserved.\nReleased under the MIT license as described in the file LICENSE.\n\n# The empty-pair lemma: the deterministic core of the first-moment pathwidth bound\n\n`reports/random_graph_pathwidth_survey.md` §1.4 derives a lower bound on the\npathwidth of `G(n, c/n)` by a first moment over *empty pairs*: two disjoint\nvertex sets with no edge between them. The deterministic half of that argument\nis proved here, in the sharp form checked by brute force on every graph on at\nmost 9 vertices (`reports/scratch/empty_pair/check.py`, 2026-10-30, zero\nviolations, tight on 22.5% at 9 vertices).\n\nThe argument is one line in the repository's convention for vertex separation\n(`VertexSeparation.lean`: at position `i` the *active suffix* is the set of\nsuffix vertices with a neighbour in the prefix). At any position `i` of a layout\n`σ`, the prefix `P` and the *inactive* suffix `R` — the suffix vertices with no\nprefix neighbour — are an empty pair, `|P| = i + 1`, and `|R| ≥ n − i − 1 − k`\nwhere `k` is the layout's vertex separation. Since `R` avoids the closed\nneighbourhood `N[P]`, this says `|N[P]| ≤ |P| + k`.\n\n## Main results\n\n* `exists_prefix_closedNbhd_card_le` — for every `j ≤ n` there is a `j`-set `A`\n  with `|N[A]| ≤ j + pathwidth G`. This is Harper's vertex-isoperimetric lower\n  bound on pathwidth in its layout form (CLAUDE.md, *the expansion bound*;\n  Chandran & Kavitha 2006 on hypercubes): `pathwidth G ≥ min_{|A| = j} |N[A]| − j`\n  for every `j`.\n* `emptyPairIndex G` — `s*(G) = max_A min(|A| − 1, n − |N[A]|)`, the largest `s`\n  such that some set of at least `s + 1` vertices has at least `s` vertices\n  outside its closed neighbourhood.\n* `card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth` — **the sharp\n  empty-pair bound**: `n − 2·s*(G) − 2 ≤ pathwidth G`. Taking\n  `j = ⌈(n − k)/2⌉` in the first result gives a `j`-set with at least\n  `⌊(n − k)/2⌋ ≥ j − 1` vertices outside its closed neighbourhood, so\n  `s* ≥ j − 1`, i.e. `n − 2s* − 2 ≤ n − 2j ≤ k`.\n* `exists_empty_pair` — the survey's own phrasing: disjoint `A`, `R` with no\n  `A`–`R` edge, `|A| = ⌈(n − k)/2⌉` and `|R| ≥ ⌈(n − k)/2⌉ − 1`.\n\nThe probabilistic half (the expected number of empty pairs of these sizes in\n`G(n, c/n)` vanishes below `β*(c)·n`) is not formalised: Mathlib has no `G(n, p)`.\n-/\n\nimport MOSPFormalization.Sandwich\n\nset_option linter.unusedSectionVars false\n\nnamespace MOSPFormalization\n\nvariable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\nvariable (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n\n/-! ### Suffix cardinality (`card_prefixSet` is in `LinearLayout.lean`) -/\n\ntheorem card_suffixSet (σ : LinearLayout V) (i : ℕ) (hi : i < Fintype.card V) :\n    (suffixSet σ i).card = Fintype.card V - (i + 1) := by\n  have := card_prefixSet_add_card_suffixSet σ i rfl\n  rw [card_prefixSet σ hi] at this\n  omega\n\n/-! ### The inactive suffix -/\n\n/-- The suffix vertices at position `i` with no neighbour in the prefix. -/\ndef inactiveSuffix (σ : LinearLayout V) (i : ℕ) : Finset V :=\n  suffixSet σ i \\ activeSuffix G σ i\n\ntheorem card_inactiveSuffix (σ : LinearLayout V) (i : ℕ) :\n    (inactiveSuffix G σ i).card = (suffixSet σ i).card - vertexSepAt G σ i := by\n  unfold inactiveSuffix vertexSepAt\n  exact Finset.card_sdiff_of_subset (activeSuffix_subset_suffixSet G σ i)\n\n/-- No edge joins the prefix to the inactive suffix. -/\ntheorem not_adj_prefix_inactiveSuffix (σ : LinearLayout V) (i : ℕ) {u v : V}\n    (hu : u ∈ prefixSet σ i) (hv : v ∈ inactiveSuffix G σ i) : ¬ G.Adj u v := by\n  intro huv\n  unfold inactiveSuffix at hv\n  rw [Finset.mem_sdiff] at hv\n  apply hv.2\n  rw [mem_activeSuffix_iff]\n  exact ⟨(mem_suffixSet_iff σ v i).mp hv.1, u, hu, huv⟩\n\"\"\"Q2(a): is P(fixed pair (A, R) empty) <= prod_j q(s_j) under the real generator?\nFor each cell (n = m, d), generator and t: N instances; per instance P random disjoint pairs with |A| = t,\n|R| = t - 1; the fraction of empty pairs against prod_j q(s_j) from the instance's realised sizes.\nRatio = mean(empty fraction) / mean(prod q), bootstrap CI over instances.  Also the conditional version:\nratio within the instances whose total ones is in the lower / upper half (does the direction depend on sizes?).\n    nice -n 19 python3 mc_empty_pairs.py GEN1,GEN2,... [N] [P]\n\"\"\"\nimport os, sys, math, time, csv\nimport numpy as np\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom multiprocessing import Pool\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\nfrom gen_candidates import CANDIDATES\nfrom bound_lib import lq\n\ndef uniform_given_sizes(n, m, d, rng, base):\n    \"\"\"control: an instance from `base`, every pattern redrawn as a uniform subset of its realised size.\"\"\"\n    M, t = CANDIDATES[base](n, m, d, rng)\n    sizes = M.sum(axis=1)\n    U = np.zeros_like(M)\n    for i, s in enumerate(sizes):\n        U[i, rng.choice(n, int(s), replace=False)] = True\n    return U, t\n\ndef t_list(n, d, m):\n    \"\"\"t values at which prod q >= 1e-6 for patterns of size d (direct estimation feasible), plus t = 2.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for t in range(2, n // 2 + 1):\n        if m * lq(n, t, d) >= math.log(1e-6): out.append(t)\n    return out\n\ndef job(args):\n    cell, gen, N, P, seed = args\n    n, m, d = cell\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    ts = t_list(n, d, m)\n    emp = {t: [] for t in ts}; qs = {t: [] for t in ts}; ones = []\n    t0 = time.time()\n    for _ in range(N):\n        if gen.startswith(\"uniform:\"):\n            M, _ = uniform_given_sizes(n, m, d, rng, gen.split(\":\", 1)[1])\n        else:\n            M, _ = CANDIDATES[gen](n, m, d, rng)\n        if M is None: continue\n        Mi = M.astype(np.float32)\n        sizes = M.sum(axis=1); ones.append(int(sizes.sum()))\n        for t in ts:\n            # P random pairs: a random permutation per pair, A = first t, R = next t - 1\n            perm = rng.random((P, n)).argsort(axis=1)\n            A = np.zeros((n, P), np.float32); R = np.zeros((n, P), np.float32)\n            cols = np.arange(P)\n            for k in range(t): A[perm[:, k], cols] = 1\n            for k in range(t, 2 * t - 1): R[perm[:, k], cols] = 1\n            meetsA = (Mi @ A) > 0; meetsR = (Mi @ R) > 0\n            empty = ~(meetsA & meetsR).any(axis=0)\n            emp[t].append(empty.mean())\n            qs[t].append(math.exp(sum(lq(n, t, int(s)) for s in sizes)))\n    rows = []\n    ones = np.array(ones); med = np.median(ones)\n    for t in ts:\n        e = np.array(emp[t]); q = np.array(qs[t])\n        if e.mean() == 0: \n            rows.append(dict(n=n, m=m, d=d, gen=gen, t=t, N=len(e), P=P, p_emp=0.0, q_mean=q.mean(), ratio=0.0, lo=0.0, hi=0.0, ratio_lowones=math.nan, ratio_highones=math.nan, hits=0))\n            continue\n        bs = []\n        for _ in range(1000):\n            idx = rng.integers(0, len(e), len(e)); bs.append(e[idx].mean() / q[idx].mean())\n        lo, hi = np.percentile(bs, [2.5, 97.5])\n        low = ones <= med; high = ones > med\n        rows.append(dict(n=n, m=m, d=d, gen=gen, t=t, N=len(e), P=P, p_emp=e.mean(), q_mean=q.mean(), ratio=e.mean() / q.mean(), lo=lo, hi=hi,\n                         ratio_lowones=e[low].mean() / q[low].mean() if low.any() else math.nan,\n                         ratio_highones=e[high].mean() / q[high].mean() if high.any() and q[high].mean() > 0 else math.nan,\n                         hits=int(e.sum() * P)))\n    return rows, time.time() - t0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    gens = sys.argv[1].split(\",\")\n    N = int(sys.argv[2]) if len(sys.argv) > 2 else 1000\n    P = int(sys.argv[3]) if len(sys.argv) > 3 else 2000\n    cells = [(n, n, d) for n in (30, 50) for d in (2, 4, 6)]\n    jobs = [(c, g, N, P, abs(hash((c, g))) % 2**32) for c in cells for g in gens]\n    allrows = []\n    with Pool(int(os.environ.get(\"NPROC\", \"8\"))) as pool:\n        for rows, secs in pool.imap_unordered(job, jobs):\n            allrows += rows\n            for r in rows:\n                print(f\"({r['n']},{r['m']},{r['d']}) {r['gen']:28s} t={r['t']:2d} p_emp={r['p_emp']:.3e} prod_q={r['q_mean']:.3e} ratio={r['ratio']:.3f} [{r['lo']:.3f}, {r['hi']:.3f}] low/high-ones {r['ratio_lowones']:.3f}/{r['ratio_highones']:.3f} hits={r['hits']}\", flush=True)\n            print(f\"  ({secs:.0f} s)\", flush=True)\n    with open(HERE / \"mc_empty_pairs.csv\", \"a\", newline=\"\") as f:\n        w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=list(allrows[0].keys()))\n        if f.tell() == 0: w.writeheader()\n        w.writerows(allrows)\n\"\"\"loop0010 item 02: `learning.first_moment` reproduces the two scratch logs\n(`reports/scratch/clique_cover_moment/asymptotic_cs.log`, `finite_bound_cs.log`)\nto their printed digits, and its histogram interface agrees with the list one.\"\"\"\n\nimport math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom learning.first_moment import (CS_DIR, beta_star, chu_stuckey_sizes, floors, log_bound, rate, threshold_d)\n\n# fixed size d, mu = m/n: beta*(d, mu), the table of asymptotic_cs.log (3 digits)\nASYMPTOTIC_TABLE = {\n    2: (0.000, 0.000, 0.121, 0.307),\n    3: (0.000, 0.140, 0.355, 0.570),\n    4: (0.072, 0.286, 0.525, 0.715),\n    5: (0.158, 0.411, 0.642, 0.799),\n    6: (0.244, 0.513, 0.724, 0.851),\n    8: (0.398, 0.661, 0.825, 0.909),\n    10: (0.522, 0.757, 0.880, 0.939),\n    15: (0.726, 0.877, 0.942, 0.971),\n    20: (0.832, 0.927, 0.965, 0.982),\n}\nMUS = (0.5, 1, 2, 4)\n\n# class (n, m, d): beta* (empirical sizes) from asymptotic_cs.log, and the mean\n# floors (< 1, < 0.01) from finite_bound_cs.log\nCLASS_LOG = {\n    (30, 30, 2): (0.097, 5.4, 4.6),\n    (50, 100, 2): (0.277, 16.6, 15.8),\n    (75, 75, 6): (0.538, 44.4, 43.2),\n    (100, 50, 4): (0.133, 17.0, 15.4),\n    (125, 125, 10): (0.762, 99.6, 98.0),\n}\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"d\", sorted(ASYMPTOTIC_TABLE))\ndef test_beta_star_table_matches_the_scratch_log(d):\n    for mu, expected in zip(MUS, ASYMPTOTIC_TABLE[d]):\n        assert f\"{beta_star(mu, [d]):.3f}\" == f\"{expected:.3f}\"\n\n\ndef test_threshold_in_d_matches_the_scratch_log():\n    assert f\"{threshold_d():.4f}\" == \"2.0031\"\n\n\ndef test_beta_star_is_positive_exactly_above_excess_one():\n    # fixed sizes: e = mu (d - 1); the closed-form threshold is e = 1\n    assert beta_star(1.0, [2]) == 0.0           # e = 1\n    assert beta_star(0.5, [3]) == 0.0           # e = 1\n    assert beta_star(0.5, [4]) > 0.0            # e = 1.5\n    assert beta_star(2.0, [2]) > 0.0            # e = 2\n\n\ndef test_rate_accepts_a_histogram_and_agrees_with_the_list():\n    sizes = [2, 2, 3, 5, 5, 5, 8]\n    hist = {2: 2, 3: 1, 5: 3, 8: 1}\n    for a in (0.05, 0.2, 0.4):\n        assert rate(a, 1.3, hist) == pytest.approx(rate(a, 1.3, sizes), abs=1e-12)\n    assert beta_star(1.3, hist) == pytest.approx(beta_star(1.3, sizes), abs=1e-12)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fa27ee1-b847-4ca1-8871-482bf0608ed2","uuid":"79de9c5f-cfe8-4c31-9870-9d97e3f02686","timestamp":"2026-11-01T04:28:58.126Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"9:## 1. The map: every instance on record placed by its excess (item 01, 2026-10-31)\n49:### 1.1 Findings\n144:### 1.2 Kill verdict\n153:### 1.3 What this does not claim\n164:### 1.4 Figure\n174:### 1.5 Tables, in full\n887:## 2. `opt / n` against the excess: one parameter, or two? (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n935:### 2.1 Findings\n1024:### 2.2 Kill verdict\n1035:### 2.3 What this does not claim\n1055:### 2.4 Figure\n1068:### 2.5 Tables, in full\n1282:## 3. The forest regime, by brute force (item 03, 2026-11-01)\n1378:### 3.1 Findings\n1484:### 3.2 Kill verdict\n1504:### 3.3 What this does not claim\n1526:### 3.4 Tables, in full\n1881:### 3.5 The two censored random block trees, rerun for an hour each\n1903:## 4. Industrial instances: the 